    "https://assets.sfc.edu/content/uploads/Associate-Director-of-Academic-Career-Development-Job.pdf": {
      "title": "St. Francis College invites applicants for the position of Associate Director of Academic and Career",
      "description": " \n \nAbout St. Francis College (SFC) \nFounded in 1859 by the Franciscan Brothers, St. Francis College (SFC) has a rich legacy of academic \nexcellence and service. Located in Brooklyn, New York, SFC educates over 4,500 students, primarily from \nthe five boroughs, with backgrounds representing over 70 countries and more than 40 languages. With a \nnetwork of 20,000 alumni, the college has produced distinguished public servants, scientists, lawyers, \nbusiness professionals, nurses, teachers, and members of the clergy. \nRooted in Franciscan values of community, collaboration, and connection, SFC remains committed to \nproviding a first -class, attainable, high-quality education to individuals of all racial, ethnic, and religious \nbackgrounds, with a special focus on working- class New Yorkers. In 2021, the College launched a \ntransformative initiative to restructure academic and programmatic offerings, aligning them with \n21stcentury career needs while expanding its reach locally and internationally. In 2022, SFC relocated to \na modern, state-of-the-art campus in Downtown Brooklyn, ensuring it continues to prepare students for \nsuccess in a rapidly evolving world. \n \nJob Summary: \nSt. Francis College invites applicants for the position of Associate Director of Academic and Career \nDevelopment. Reporting to the Director of Academic and Career Advisement, the Associate Director \nwill play a critical role in advancing the integration of academic advising and career development across \nthe student lifecycle. \nWe seek a strategic, student-centered professional committed to strengthening academic and career \npathways, enhancing career readiness, and improving post-graduate outcomes for students and \nalumni. The successful candidate will contribute to a collaborative campus environment through \ninnovative programming, effective use of technology, employer engagement, and cross-divisional \npartnerships that support student success and align with the mission and values of St. Francis College. \nSalary Range: $70,000 - $80,000 per year \n \n \nEssential Duties and Responsibilities: Associate Director \n \n• Design and implement a clear, integrated career pathway that supports students \nthroughout their academic journey at St. Francis College. \n• Strengthen collaboration between students and academic advisors to align academic \nplanning with career readiness and post-graduate outcomes. \n• Foster deeper connections between academic advising, career development, and \nemployer engagement. \n• Create and deliver career workshops, webinars, seminars, and networking events for \nstudents, graduating seniors, recent graduates, and alumni. \n \n• Assist students and alumni in evaluating career goals and developing multi-faceted job and \ninternship search strategies.  \n• Track and assess student outcomes and alumni experiences to inform continuous \nimprovement of academic and career initiatives. \n• Serve as a liaison to alumni affairs, corporate affairs, College advancement, academic \nfaculty, and alumni to co-create targeted career programming. \n• Assist with the planning and execution of large- and small-scale events, including career \nfairs, networking nights, orientations, and related initiatives. \n• Expand and optimize the use of career and advising technologies, including Handshake, \nNavigate, Forage, Indeed, and Self Service, to support student success. \n• Streamline technology use and communication strategies for students, faculty, and staff. \nContribute to assessment, divisional initiatives, and other duties as assigned. \n \nRequired Qualifications \n• Bachelor’s degree with a minimum of five (5) years of professional experience in academic \nand career counseling, higher education career development, recruiting, or executive \ncoaching. \n• Experience working with First Generation students and First Generation Centers. \n• Demonstrated knowledge of current labor market trends and local, regional, and national \njob search strategies. \n• Excellent interpersonal, presentation, and teaching skills. \n• Strong organizational, event, and program management abilities, with attention to detail \nand systems-building. \n• Ability to work collaboratively in a fast-paced, team-oriented environment. \n• Experience working with and affirming individuals from diverse cultural and socioeconomic \nbackgrounds. \n• Demonstrated cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for a multicultural college \ncommunity and the Franciscan traditions of St. Francis College. \n• Strong commitment to the mission of St. Francis College and to supporting its students, \nfaculty, and staff. \n \nPreferred Qualifications \n• Master’s degree in a related field. \n• Experience using academic and career management platforms such as Handshake, \nNavigate, Forage, Indeed, and Self Service. \n• Strong technology skills, including proficiency with Microsoft Office and online \ncommunication and collaboration tools. \n• Experience developing and delivering career programming, workshops, and employer-\nfacing initiatives. \n \nInterested applicants can apply by clicking here. \n \nSt. Francis College is committed to providing opportunities to all persons without regard to \nsex, race, creed, color, religion, national origin, citizenship status, age, disability, marital \nstatus, gender identity or expression, predisposing genetic characteristics, status as a \nvictim of domestic violence, sexual orientation, status as a Vietnam -era or special disabled \nveteran, or any other characteristic protected by law in its education programs and activities \nor employment. \nThe College is an Equal Opportunity employer. "
    },
    "https://assets.sfc.edu/content/uploads/Adjunct_Faculty_JobDescription.pdf": {
      "title": "Reporting to the Department Chair, the Adjunct should have the ability and willingness to teach within",
      "description": " \n \nAbout St. Francis College (SFC) \n \nFounded in 1859 by the Franciscan Brothers, St. Francis College (SFC) has a rich legacy of academic \nexcellence and service. Located in Brooklyn, New York, SFC educates over 4,500 students, primarily from \nthe five boroughs, with backgrounds representing over 70 countries and more than 40 languages. With a \nnetwork of 20,000 alumni, the college has produced distinguished public servants, scientists, lawyers, \nbusiness professionals, nurses, teachers, and members of the clergy. \n \nRooted in Franciscan values of community, collaboration, and connection, SFC remains committed to \nproviding a first-class, attainable, high-quality education to individuals of all racial, ethnic, and religious \nbackgrounds, with a special focus on working-class New Yorkers. In 2021, the College launched a \ntransformative initiative to restructure academic and programmatic offerings, aligning them with 21st-\ncentury career needs while expanding its reach locally and internationally. In 2022, SFC relocated to a \nmodern, state-of-the-art campus in Downtown Brooklyn, ensuring it continues to prepare students for \nsuccess in a rapidly evolving world. \n \nJob Summary: \nReporting to the Department Chair, the Adjunct should have the ability and willingness to teach within \ntheir field, a wide range of introductory and intermediate undergraduate courses, and to a lesser extent, \ngraduate courses. \nSalary Range: $1,605 - $1,730 per credit (Adjunct and Senior Adjunct) \n \nEssential Duties & Responsibilities: \n• Teach assigned courses following College-approved curricula and standards. \n• Develop and update course materials, including syllabi and assessments. \n• Engage students through effective instruction and active learning strategies. \n• Assess student performance and provide timely feedback. \n• Maintain office hours as required for student support. \n• Utilize learning management systems (e.g., Canvas) for course delivery. \n• Adhere to academic policies and institutional guidelines. \n• Participate in departmental meetings and professional development as needed. \n• Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence and student success. \nPotential Teaching Disciplines: \n• Communication Arts \n• English \n• Philosophy & Religious Studies \n• Accounting & Business Law \n• Management & Information Technology \n• Biology & Health Sciences \n• Chemistry & Physics \n• Mathematics \n• Nursing \n• Economics, History & Political Science \n• Education \n• Psychology \n• Sociology & Criminal Justice \n• Foreign Languages, Fine Arts & International Cultural Studies \n \n \nMinimum Qualifications: \n \n• Master’s degree in a relevant academic discipline from an accredited institution is required. \n• Experience with online and hybrid teaching methodologies preferred. \n• Commitment to undergraduate teaching and global awareness is required. \n• Candidates selected for an interview must provide three letters of reference. \nInterested applicants can apply by clicking here. \n \nSt. Francis College is committed to providing opportunities to all persons without regard to sex, race, \ncreed, color, religion, national origin, citizenship status, age, disability, marital status, gender identity or \nexpression, predisposing genetic characteristics, status as a victim of domestic violence, sexual \norientation, status as a Vietnam-era or special disabled veteran, or any other characteristic protected by \nlaw in its education programs and activities or employment.  \nThe College is an Equal Opportunity employer. \n "
    },
    "https://assets.sfc.edu/content/uploads/Graduate-Adjunct-Faculty__rev.2024.pdf": {
      "title": "Reporting to the Department Chair, the Adjunct will teach in various formats, including in-person, hybrid,",
      "description": " \n \n \n \nAbout St. Francis College (SFC) \n \nFounded in 1859 by the Franciscan Brothers, St. Francis College (SFC) has a rich legacy of academic \nexcellence and service. Located in Brooklyn, New York, SFC educates over 4,500 students, primarily from \nthe ﬁve boroughs, with backgrounds representing over 70 countries and more than 40 languages. With a \nnetwork of 20,000 alumni, the college has produced distinguished public servants, scientists, lawyers, \nbusiness professionals, nurses, teachers, and members of the clergy. \n \nRooted in Franciscan values of community, collaboration, and connection, SFC remains committed to \nproviding a ﬁrst-class, attainable, high-quality education to individuals of all racial, ethnic, and religious \nbackgrounds, with a special focus on working-class New Yorkers. In 2021, the College launched a \ntransformative initiative to restructure academic and programmatic oﬀerings, aligning them with 21st-\ncentury career needs while expanding its reach locally and internationally. In 2022, SFC relocated to a \nmodern, state-of-the-art campus in Downtown Brooklyn, ensuring it continues to prepare students for \nsuccess in a rapidly evolving world. \n \nJob Summary: \nReporting to the Department Chair, the Adjunct will teach in various formats, including in-person, hybrid, \nand online. We seek to hire candidates with a minimum of three (3) years of teaching experience for the \nsuccessful and expanding programs included below. \n \nSalary Range: $1,605 per credit - $1,730 per credit (Adjunct and Senior Adjunct) \n \nEssential Duties & Responsibilities: \n \n• Teach assigned courses following College-approved curricula and standards. \n• Develop and update course materials, including syllabi and assessments. \n• Engage students through effective instruction and active learning strategies. \n• Assess student performance and provide timely feedback. \n• Maintain office hours as required for student support. \n• Utilize learning management systems (e.g., Canvas) for course delivery. \n• Adhere to academic policies and institutional guidelines. \n• Participate in departmental meetings and professional development as needed. \n• Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence and student success. \nPotential Teaching Disciplines: \nM.S. in Management  \n• MGT-6001 Organization and Strategy - understand dynamic organizations as evolving systems \nthrough literature, case studies, and class discussions \n• MKT-6001 Marketing for Managers - explore strategic planning, ethical issues, consumer behavior, \ndecision-making in contemporary, customer-driven positioning strategies, and activities of the \nmarketing mix \n• PM-6000 Fundamentals of Project Management - learn business project management guidelines in \nany industry and how to address a range of project challenges through team exercise settings \n• BAN-6001 Business Analysis for Managers - business applications of probability and statistics, \nforecasting techniques, and decision theory \n \n \n \n• BAN-6300 Emerging Issues of Technical Analytics - data presentation, governance, sharing, modeling, \nmining, forecasting, ethics, literacy, quality, machine learning, data visualization, predictive analytics, \nbig data, decision analysis \n• ACC-6650 Elementary Accounting - introduction to the accounting cycle, financial statements, current \nassets, liabilities, and non-current assets \n• FIN-6001 Corporate Finance - analyze discounted cash flow techniques, corporate capital budgeting, \ninvestment decisions, financial asset pricing, and theory of market efficiency \n \nM.S. Information Technology  \n• IT-6001 Information Systems for Management - use of planning, decision making, adequate controls, \nand business operations to transform business operations \n• IT-6002 System Analysis and Design - how computer systems are analyzed and designed, analysis \nrequirements and process, design essentials, quality assurance, and implementation \n• IT-6003 Computer Network and Security - introduction to computer networks, applications, link, \nphysical, network, and transport layers, cyber security threats and defenses \n• IT-6005 Operating Systems - cover basic operating system structure, file systems and storage servers, \nmemory management techniques, process scheduling and resource management, threads, \ndistributed and peer-to-peer systems, and security  \n \nM.S. in Computer Science \n• CS-6001 Programming, Data, Algorithms - programming and data structures using strings, integers, \nBooleans, floats, stacks, queues, and arrays \n• CS-6002 Computer Organization Architecture - overview of CPU architecture, memory hierarchies, \ninput/output processes, hardware components, and software interactions to execute instructions \n• CS-6003 Algorithm Design Analysis - designing algorithms in terms of time and space that address \nchallenges and efficiency, optimization techniques, challenges, and resource constraints \n• CS-6004 Programming Languages - overview of SQL, Python, Java, Javascript, C++, etc. to understand \nlanguage paradigms, syntax, semantics, and use in development environments \n• CS-6006 Software Engineering - design, development, testing, methodologies, maintenance, quality \nassurance, and tools to deliver robust and maintainable software \n \nMinimum Qualifications: \n \n• A master’s degree from an accredited institution in a relevant academic discipline is required. \n• Experience with online and hybrid teaching methodologies is preferred, including Canvas LMS, \nMicrosoft Teams, and Yuja videos. \n• Must attend the in-person Residency Program for at least three dates each semester (specific dates \nwill be provided upon hiring). \n• A commitment to graduate teaching and global awareness is required. \n• Candidates selected for an interview must provide three letters of reference. \n• A strong commitment to the College’s mission and its students, faculty, and staff is essential. \n \nInterested applicants can apply by clicking here. \n \nSt. Francis College is committed to providing opportunities to all persons without regard to sex, race, \ncreed, color, religion, national origin, citizenship status, age, disability, marital status, gender identity or \nexpression, predisposing genetic characteristics, status as a victim of domestic violence, sexual \norientation, status as a Vietnam-era or special disabled veteran, or any other characteristic protected by \nlaw in its education programs and activities or employment.  \nThe College is an Equal Opportunity employer. "
    }
  },
